<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qizilbash</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Qizilbash were a confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribes devoted to the Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> order, named after their distinctive red headgear. They brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> to power in 1501 and formed the core military and political elite of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>. Their factionalism later led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> to counterbalance them with a corps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulam</a> slave soldiers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qizilbash.jpg" alt="Qizilbash" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Circa 1647 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qizilbash_troops_of_Shah_Ismail_I._Circa_1647_painting,_Chehel_Sotoun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name comes from Turkish and means red head, after a crimson cap of twelve gores which marked adherence to the Twelve Imams and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-haydar">Shaykh Haydar</a>, head of the Safavid order. It began as a pejorative used by their Sunni Ottoman enemies and was taken up as a mark of pride. In the 15th century Haydar organised his followers into militant troops. They were at first drawn from seven Turkish-speaking tribes: the Rumlu, Shamlu, Ustajlu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afshar-tribe">Afshar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a>, Tekelu and Zulkadar. Their beliefs were heterodox: they regarded their Safavid leader as a manifestation of the divine, and orthodox Twelver Shia classed them as ghulat, extremists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the summer of 1500 about 7,000 supporters had rallied to the young Ismail at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a>. They overcame a force of 30,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>. In 1510 the Qizilbash defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> at Marv, but in 1512 an army was destroyed at the Battle of Ghazdewan after Turcoman Qizilbash mutinied against their Persian commander, Najm-e Thani. On 20 August 1514 the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a>, whose army had firearms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and was reported to outnumber them as much as three to one, defeated them badly at Chaldiran. The defeat destroyed Ismail's belief in his own invincibility and changed his relation to his followers.</p><p>For almost ten years afterwards rival Qizilbash factions fought for control of the kingdom, and the ten-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Tahmasp I</a>, who succeeded in 1524, was at first the ward of the Qizilbash amir Ali Beg Rumlu. Tahmasp reasserted his authority, and the Qizilbash defended the kingdom against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-amasya">Peace of Amasya</a> in 1555. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Abbas I</a>, who came to power in 1588, built a standing army and administration from Circassian, Georgian and Armenian ghulams and ended the independent rule of Turcoman chiefs in the provinces. By the end of his reign a fifth of the high-ranking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a> were ghulams.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Qizilbash remained part of the Safavid army and administration, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afshar-tribe">Afshar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> rulers who followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> came from a Qizilbash background. By the 18th century the name was applied broadly to anyone in the service of the Safavid state. Many Qizilbash were settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> during the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>, and others joined the Mughal court. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> the Qizilbash live mainly in cities such as Kabul, Kandahar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>; estimates of their number range from 30,000 to 200,000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qizilbash” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shah Ismail I was a grandson of Uzun Hasan through his daughter, and the Safavids took over the Aq Qoyunlu capital at Tabriz, much of their territory, their Turkmen tribal army, which became the core of the <b>Qizilbash</b>, and many of their Persian bureaucrats and administrative practices.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its core was 10,000 mounted ghulams, slave-soldiers carrying the sher-bacha blunderbuss, a third of them former Shia <b>Qizilbash</b> soldiers of Nader Shah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He led the <b>Qizilbash</b>, a confederation of Turkmen tribes (traditionally seven) named for their distinctive red headgear, to victory over the Aq Qoyunlu at the Battle of Sharur, seized Tabriz and proclaimed himself Shah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Jabani in 1500 his <b>Qizilbash</b> army defeated and killed Farrukh Yasar, and Ismail then took Shamakhi and Baku.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Turkoman <b>Qizilbash</b> tribes, the “men of the sword”, had brought the dynasty to power in 1501 and governed most of the provinces; Persians, the “men of the pen”, staffed the bureaucracy and the religious establishment.</blockquote><a href="/societies/safavid-iran">Social Hierarchy of Safavid Iran</a></li></ul></section>
